<!--
  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS — 2026-08-10.

  ProjectSettings has stripEngineCode: 1, so Unity registers only the native
  engine classes it can SEE referenced. UnityEngine.CapsuleCollider is never
  named by any line of managed code in this project, and it was therefore
  stripped from every player build. Measured in the shipped Xcode export
  rather than inferred:

      build/ios/Il2CppOutputProject/Source/il2cppOutput/UnityClassRegistration.cpp

  registers BoxCollider, Collider, MeshCollider and SphereCollider under
  "Physics" — and no CapsuleCollider. Device and simulator builds alike. The
  built app then said, from first launch, before any input, repeatedly:

      Can't add component because class 'CapsuleCollider' doesn't exist!

  IT IS REQUESTED BECAUSE OF A UNITY QUIRK. GameObject.CreatePrimitive(
  PrimitiveType.Cylinder) attaches a CAPSULECOLLIDER — the cylinder is the odd
  primitive out (Cube -> Box, Sphere -> Sphere, Plane -> Mesh). So the type is
  demanded only at runtime and is invisible to a source grep, which is why this
  looked like a phantom for a day. There are 33 live CreatePrimitive(Cylinder)
  sites: PartVisualFactory (25), ArenaHazards (6), BuilderManager (2).

  MOST OF THOSE 33 ARE HARMLESS NOISE — PartVisualFactory.Deco and the
  decorative ArenaHazards.Prim(..., collider: false) sites destroy the collider
  on the next line, and with the class stripped GetComponent returns null and
  Destroy(null) is a no-op, so the end state is identical.

  ONE IS NOT NOISE, AND IT IS WHY THIS FILE IS A FIX RATHER THAN A MUTE:

      ArenaHazards.cs, PillarHazard.Build() — Prim(..., collider: true)
      "Static pillar: an obstacle, never a damager. Real collider."

  It is the only cylinder in the project that KEEPS its collider. Without this
  preservation the yard's two pillars are created with no collider at all and
  robots drive straight through them — silently, with no exception beyond the
  spam above. The yard is L1 "Scrapyard Open", the FIRST league in the game, and
  ArenaHazards.Summary("yard") returns the literal string "pillars", which the
  league card renders: the app advertised an obstacle it did not have, in the
  first fights every new player sees.

  WHY NO BENCH CAUGHT IT: HazardBench is 23/23 green in the EDITOR, and the
  editor does not strip engine code. This class of defect is invisible to every
  bench in this project by construction — it can only be seen in a built player.

  WHY NOT AN EXPLICIT AddComponent<CapsuleCollider>() IN PillarHazard: it was
  tried first and measured, and it is worse on two counts. Unity's default
  capsule is height 1 while CreatePrimitive's is height 2, so the pillar would
  silently get a collider 60% of its height; and it requires collider: false,
  whose Object.Destroy is deferred in play mode and a no-op in edit mode,
  leaving two overlapping capsules. The numbers are in PillarHazard's comment.

  HOW TO VERIFY THIS IS WORKING: after a build, that generated
  UnityClassRegistration.cpp must contain

      RegisterUnityClass<CapsuleCollider>("Physics");

  If it does not, this file is not being picked up and the pillars are hollow
  again. That check is the whole point; a green editor bench cannot replace it.

  SCOPE: deliberately one type. This is not a blanket "preserve physics" — the
  other collider types are named in managed code and survive on their own, and
  a broad preserve would hide the next instance of exactly this bug.
-->
<linker>
  <assembly fullname="UnityEngine.PhysicsModule">
    <type fullname="UnityEngine.CapsuleCollider" preserve="all" />
  </assembly>
</linker>
